==English==
===Etymology===
From past participle of Old French verb, from Late Latin ''[[ex]]'', "out", "away", + verb ''[[cadere]]'', "fall".

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{legal}} The [[return]] of [[property]] of a [[deceased]] person to the [[state]] (originally to a [[feudal]] [[lord]]) where there are no [[legal]] [[heir]]s or [[claimant]]s.
# {{legal}} The property so [[revert]]ed.
# {{obsolete}} [[plunder|Plunder]], [[booty]].
#*'''1590''', Edmund Spenser, ''The Faerie Queene'', III.viii:
#*: Approching, with bold words and bitter threat, / Bad that same boaster, as he mote, on high / To leaue to him that Lady for '''excheat''', / Or bide him battell without further treat.

* {{seeCites}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# to revert by this process

